{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'function Show($p,$id){$l=Get-Content $p;$s=($l|Select-String -SimpleMatch $id|Select-Object -First 1).LineNumber;while($l[$s-1]-notmatch '\"'\"'^'\"\\\\['){\"'$s--};$e=$s+1;while($e -le $l.Count -and $l[$e-1]-notmatch '\"'\"'^'\"\\\\['){\"'$e++};$l[($s-1)..($e-2)]}; Show docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml LLR-207; Show docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml LLR-341; Show docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml LLR-344; Show docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml LLR-313; Show docs/test/test-cases.toml TC-132; Show docs/test/test-cases.toml TC-333; Show docs/test/test-cases.toml TC-371; Show docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.toml LLR-367'","aggregated_output":"[design.LLR-207]\r\nsr_refs = [\"SR-156\"]\r\ntitle = \"The verdict record: what carries a governing verdict, and what binds it to the tree it judged\"\r\nmodule = \"project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py;project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/station.py;project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py\"\r\ncode_symbol = \"RECORD_PREFIXES/fold_listing/tree_identity/refresh_subject/refresh_attestation/format_bar_green/bar_attestation/tests_digest/mechanical_close_attestation/work_tip/governing_rev/governing_identity/format_trailer/parse_trailer/round_file/session_log/format_scope/gating_span/filing_refusal/branch_paths/logged_rounds/round_entries/branch_entries/tree_already_judged/declared_phases/phases_owed/round_count/_closed_wi_ids/mechanical_close_subject/format_branch_trailer/branch_trailers;attest/_attest_preflight/_attest_message/_lane_off_trunk/_harness_floor_refusal/_attest_commit/_bar_refusal/_review_bar_command/_run_review_bar/_unstaged_dirt\"\r\ndetail = \"\"\"tree_identity folds raw NUL-delimited `git ls-tree -r -z` entries after dropping docs/reviews/, docs/log.d/ and docs/iteration/, and returns a 64-hex SHA-256 identity. fold_listing accepts bytes and preserves every path byte so display quoting or decoding cannot merge distinct work trees. A Bar-Green attestation is exactly one column-0 label line, `Bar-Green: tree=<40 hex> work=<40 hex> bar=<review|landing|no-bar> tests=<64 hex|none> <summary>`; its verifier returns fields only when tree and work equal the commit's own tree and first parent and bar is in that set. tests carries none for an empty named test-path set; otherwise it carries the SHA-256 of that set after path separators and leading ./ are normalized, duplicates are removed, and the paths are joined in sorted order. Thus one set in any order or spelling has one value, and a different set has a different value. refresh_attestation admits a refresh subject only with that verified attestation and bar landing or no-bar, so work_tip never peels a review attestation as a refresh; the read-only identity walk still steps through any commit whose non-record identity equals its parent, including an empty review attestation. One producer writes every attestation: it fixes the staged tree before the bar, refuses a red bar or one that leaves the index or worktree off that tree, and restores the index on every failed write; its line joins the message's final trailer block. The attest route refuses before the bar when commit hooks are off, the primary checkout holds the branch, the worktree is dirty (including untracked files), the review-bar declaration is absent or empty, the message carries the label, or the harness floor refuses; it reads the review bar and named tests from the attested lane's own declarations, with no default. A trailer without bar is not read. work_tip peels only a verified refresh at the tip for the reset path; governing_rev is read-only and walks across commits whose non-record identity equals their first parent's, peeling any DISPOSABLE commit it meets to reach one those commits would otherwise hide, and ending at the first commit whose identity differs from its parent's or at its absent-parent and depth bounds - so a branch with no disposable commit under it still walks. TWO commit classes are disposable and the read-only walk peels BOTH: the station's REFRESH, admitted by refresh_attestation, and the machinery's OWN adjudication close, admitted by mechanical_close_attestation against exactly one parent and a no-renames changed-path stream lying wholly under docs/work/. That stream must pair deleted specs from exactly ONE docs/work/active/<branch>/ with additions of the same filenames under docs/work/complete/, and ONLY the move itself may create or destroy: an addition or deletion under docs/work/ that is not one half of a recognised move refuses the whole commit, since peeling it would measure a spec's injection or an archived row's destruction at the parent, where no reviewer saw it. Modifications ride along unrestricted, because a close's inbound relink rewrites other rows in place and only ever modifies. A mechanical close admits exactly one moved row; any other shape declines to peel toward more review. Its subject must equal the writer's composed subject exactly, so a public prefix/suffix with a forged middle is not an attestation. The one-row clause refuses an empty close, which has no deletion, and every multi-row close; one deletion implies one source branch; and the subject composer takes a single id and refuses anything else, so no empty-id subject is ever composed. One property admits the two classes and it is not two rules: both are machine-authored end to end, and both move the tree without the lane having changed what it CLAIMS - docs/work/ is deliberately inside the identity because a spec's own claims are what a verdict is about, and the close carries those claims through unchanged. Every arm of both verifications fails toward REVIEW, since declining to peel measures at a later rev and can never ask for LESS - though not always for more: on the empty close the refused commit is identity-preserving, so the walk-through step reaches the same rev the peel would have, and the refusal guards this function's contract rather than any answer the gate gives. The two peels are NOT the same peel: work_tip calls refresh_attestation directly rather than through the shared disposable test, so a mechanical close is never removed by the destructive reset path, which may only ever drop a refresh sitting literally on the tip. governing_identity composes governing_rev and tree_identity for the branch tip or an explicit revision, including a round's reviewed commit and a trailer's carrier; the branch argument is a branch NAME, because the peel verifies a refresh commit against the branch it names and no refresh subject names HEAD. format_scope records a REVIEW session scope as full, narrow or final base..sha; gating_span admits a fresh full scope in either mode, or a final scope only where reviewer retention is on and final_reviewer is persisted; a narrow scope and a retained full scope never admit. logged_rounds joins branch-scoped round files to coordinator session logs by train and ordinal, and each round's review phase is the JOINED LOG's declared phase rather than the one its own filename carries, so a file cannot claim a phase its session did not serve; a joined key whose logs declare more than one review phase yields no round. It retains only a gating span whose base equals the caller's claim base and whose reviewed sha the round file names. round_entries retains only rounds whose reviewed revision governs the identity under judgement and emits phase, ordinal and verdict entries through an injected parser. filing_refusal applies one filing check: the first physical line is exactly the Reviewed full-sha opener (a CR ending is allowed; no leading blank or whitespace is), exactly one canonical VERDICT line, a finding count matching the file, and a change request naming a finding. An unreadable or refused round is an entry with no verdict, so it is the phase's latest word rather than an older verdict being used. tree_already_judged is true only for an entry carrying a verdict and false with no base. declared_phases clamps the configured independent-review count to the available review phases. phases_owed returns declared phases whose latest entry is missing or invalid evidence; a dissent is served. round_count returns the largest per-phase entry count at one governing identity, so a multi-phase review cycle counts once and changed work starts a new count. format_trailer and parse_trailer carry `Review-Verdict: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED rounds=<N> tree=<64 hex>`. branch_trailers verifies each carrier against its governing identity and returns the ordered attestation sequence per tree. Readers treat unavailable repository answers according to their own fail-closed contract.\"\"\"\r\nrationale = \"\"\"The merge gate asks whether a lane may merge while the loop asks which review phase the lane still owes. A shared identity, evidence join, phase span and count keep both answers on the same tree and policy. Logged round evidence is authoritative because its coordinator session establishes reviewer provenance; the self-verifying trailer is only a cross-check and cannot create an approval. Identity binding answers staleness directly and prevents ordering from promoting a verdict for different work. Separate read-only and reset peels protect both contracts: the reader may traverse identity-preserving commits, while the destructive reset path may remove only the verified refresh at its tip.\"\"\"\r\ntest_refs = \"TC-205; TC-132; TC-417\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nphase = 5\r\n\r\n[design.LLR-341]\r\nsr_refs = [\"SR-241\"]\r\ntitle = \"Verified review-record search before review rendering\"\r\nmodule = \"project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/verdict.py;project-trajectory/scripts/review_brief.py\"\r\ncode_symbol = \"read_bar_attestation/review_attestation/_identity_step;_review_bar_refusal\"\r\ndetail = \"The review render resolves the reviewed commit and searches that commit, then its first-parent ancestors only while their non-record identities remain equal, for one verified review-bar record whose test digest equals the digest of the round's named tests. Equivalent spellings and orderings of a named test set yield the same digest. It does not peel a disposable commit. An absent, malformed or unverified record, another bar, another test set, a changed non-record path, or no verified record among the first 64 commits the search examines, the reviewed commit included, produces a refusal; a landing or no-bar record is another-bar refusal. The refusal reports the reviewed commit, cause, both bar values for another-bar refusal, both digests for a test-set mismatch or both values for a tree or parent mismatch, and the attest step with the lane and round test list; it writes no brief and returns the refusal result. A successful render reports the attested commit and digest. The search reads the record and never runs the bar.\"\r\nrationale = \"One shared record reader keeps each review route from assigning its own meaning to a verified review result. Searching only through record-only ancestors preserves a valid result across review bookkeeping while stopping at work that can invalidate it; refusing rather than re-running preserves the separation between recording a result and deciding whether it is usable.\"\r\ntest_refs = \"TC-371; TC-419\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nphase = 6\r\n\r\n[design.LLR-344]\r\nsr_refs = [\"SR-154\"]\r\nhat_refs = [\"PERFORMANCE\"]\r\ntitle = \"Verified-attestation gate for unattended review draws\"\r\nmodule = \"project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py\"\r\ncode_symbol = \"review_draw_gate/attest_build/record_bar_result/run_iteration/worker_entry/committed_build_rounds/lane_branch/RoutingState.refuse_round\"\r\ndetail = \"A queued unattended review round is drawn only over a tip carrying a verified review attestation. A tip without one runs the review bar once through its one producer and records the result: green draws the round, while red drops it and rebuilds with the refusal. A result already recorded for the governing build commit is not produced again. The gate applies to fresh, resumed, post-narrow, and adjudication-triggered review draws.\"\r\nrationale = \"A DERIVED obligation through the PERFORMANCE lens: SR-154 requires the loop to obtain each review verdict its policy requires, but does not state the evidence condition that prevents a scarce review session from being spent on an unverified tip. A single draw gate was selected over separate route checks so recovery produces missing evidence once without either drawing over an unknown tree or duplicating a recorded result. The attended route's rendering rule remains SR-241.\"\r\ntest_refs = \"TC-375; TC-376; TC-377; TC-418\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nphase = 6\r\n\r\n[design.LLR-313]\r\nsr_refs = [\"SR-146\", \"SR-154\", \"SR-235\"]\r\ntitle = \"Attended review, critique and single-plan briefs and records\"\r\nmodule = \"project-trajectory/scripts/review_brief.py\"\r\ncode_symbol = \"Round/spec_path/round_facts/render_review/render_critique/review_refusal/round_path/file_review/lane_commits/_registry_lines/latest_critique/critique_findings/render_plan/coverage_run/render_plan_critique/file_plan_critique/pass_fields/_ruled_findings/write_pass/_critique_coverage/dispute_findings/write_dispute_findings\"\r\ndetail = \"Round accepts only a full-lane review with no prior findings or a narrow review that names the prior findings file, and requires its test list. render_review strictly fills the shipped reviewer template with the work-item specification, lane range, test command, scratch location, optional review rubric and verdict destination; render_critique strictly fills the shipped critique template with the work-item specification and named rubric. Both refuse a missing specification, unreadable or empty named input, or unfilled template slot before a brief is written. review_refusal accepts only a verdict bound to the reviewed full commit after the shared review-line reader accepts exactly one line whose keyword, in any case, is VERDICT, in exactly the canonical form `VERDICT: <APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED> findings=<digits>`, whose count matches the finding lines the review scorer reads from declared-severity brackets and whose changes request names a finding. round_path chooses the next ordinal lane round filename, file_review writes only an accepted verdict as that round record when the round reader reads its lane path back as that lane's scope, and lane_commits resolves commits on a branch and requires the base to be an ancestor of the reviewed commit. A first plan critique is bound to its named lane commit, includes coverage over the Done-when alone, opens with that commit, and refuses a missing rubric, plan or specification, a missing or beyond delta, a coverage failure, or a third round. A second plan critique is bound to its named lane commit, includes coverage over the Done-when and the first critique's finding identifiers, never their text, uses convergence stance, and refuses a revision that leaves a first-critique finding neither covered nor excluded. A single-plan brief strictly fills the shipped single-plan template with the specification, ordered Done-when clauses, required registry references, and one planner output path; a revision additionally labels the committed plan and latest filed critique findings as claims under judgement. It refuses a missing specification, Done-when, plan or critique, or an unfilled slot. A critique record is filed only when it is bound to an ancestor that holds the plan and has the verdict form; a pass record is written only from committed cited records, passing plan coverage, and a passing delta, after an approval or an accepted dismissal of every blocking and major second-round finding. At the cap, a dispute findings file is written only from the committed second critique, giving each blocking and major finding the identifier of its position among all critique findings, its text verbatim, and a declared position; it refuses another identifier set or an existing file. The second plan critique's brief never carries the first critique's verdict or any finding's text. A review round is ordinary by default or convergence: ordinary emits no stance line, while convergence emits one fixed stance line after the range and refuses a narrow scope or unknown stance before writing a brief. An ordinary review reading scope reads the delegated-decisions record and filed plan critiques, while a convergence scope excludes both; a convergence render refuses a reviewer template whose reading-scope command does not carry the review-record exclusion exactly once. The scope critique names the component and interface registries, derived architecture, runtime flows, and dependency ledger, marking an absent registry; the single-plan brief and scope critique render their registry lines from one declared set. A plan critique ties every severity class to a normal-use-reachable defect: a fail-open, data-loss, or security path counts only when a supported workflow or the kit's own routes reach it, and a rare or contrived path is MINOR at most or dismissed. Its confirming round treats a new finding as BLOCKER or MAJOR only when it is a reachable fail-open, data-loss, or Done-when gap; anything else is MINOR. A finding line bearing the convergence-class tag defined by LLR-311 is filed and counted as one finding under the unchanged verdict grammar.\"\r\nrationale = \"An attended reviewer needs the same reviewed instructions and durable verdict carrier as an unattended review without inheriting its scheduler. Strict rendering and boundary validation prevent a reviewer from receiving incomplete instructions or an unrelated verdict from becoming review history.\"\r\ntest_refs = \"TC-333; TC-419\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nphase = 6\r\n\r\n[test.TC-132]\r\nverifies = [\"SR-156\", \"LLR-140\", \"LLR-207\"]\r\nlevel = \"Unit\"\r\nmethod = \"\"\"Run the integrator suite: each claim refusal fires by name (a dirty path the claim must write among them, refused before anything moves) and the green claim moves the spec and cuts the branch; finished-branch detection keys on the tree; the verdict gate refuses absent, CHANGES-REQUESTED and stale-APPROVE verdicts and passes a fresh APPROVE; a missing or empty bar declaration refuses; the window audit flags a non-merge product commit and passes a bookkeeping-only window; an attest operation commits only verified review attestations after the bar and named-tests command declared by its lane staged tree, or refuses before that bar when hooks are off, the primary checkout holds its branch, the worktree is dirty (including untracked files), the declaration is unavailable, the harness floor refuses, or the message is unreadable or carries the label; a hook refusal or rewrite after a green bar is undone, leaving branch and index unchanged; a bar that moves the index or worktree off its staged tree is refused with the index restored; the attestation joins the message trailer block; a verified review-only tip is refused at the merge seam; one end-to-end claim->work->close->integrate run fast-forwards the trunk to a --no-ff merge commit.\"\"\"\r\ntier = \"Full\"\r\nexpected = \"Satisfies SR-156 AcceptanceCriteria via the LLR-140 claim/integration contract and the LLR-207 attest, producer and refresh-attestation contract\"\r\nautomated = \"Yes\"\r\nevidence = \"tests/test_integrate.py; tests/test_integrate_admission.py; tests/test_integrate_station.py; tests/test_integrate_attest.py\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\nphase = 4\r\n\r\n[test.TC-333]\r\nverifies = [\"SR-146\", \"SR-154\", \"SR-235\", \"LLR-313\", \"IF-046\", \"IF-047\", \"IF-288\", \"IF-289\"]\r\nlevel = \"Integration\"\r\nmethod = \"Run test_a_rendered_review_brief_carries_the_kit_clauses_and_the_lane_facts, test_a_narrow_round_names_the_findings_it_answers, test_a_narrow_round_without_findings_and_a_full_one_with_them_are_refused, test_a_named_input_that_is_not_there_refuses, test_an_unfilled_slot_refuses_and_no_brief_is_written, test_the_scope_critique_carries_ruling_twos_questions_and_its_rubric, test_a_filed_coordinator_review_appears_in_the_rollup, test_a_bracketed_word_that_is_not_a_severity_never_refuses_a_filing, test_a_malformed_review_is_refused_and_nothing_is_filed, test_a_round_with_no_tests_refuses, test_an_empty_or_missing_named_input_refuses, test_the_critique_render_refuses_a_missing_spec, test_the_command_line_refuses_a_lane_the_git_reads_reject, test_a_review_render_exits_zero_and_writes_only_the_brief, and test_filing_a_review_exits_zero_and_writes_only_the_round_file. Render full and narrow review briefs and a scope critique over a temporary lane; refuse an empty test list, empty or missing named inputs, and a critique without a specification; on a real lane, drive detached, non-commit and non-ancestor command-line refusals, then successful review rendering and filing; file a well-formed review and malformed review cases, including a non-severity bracketed bullet beside a matching declared finding count, and attempt filing for a lane whose branch the round reader cannot read back as a review scope, before rendering the rollup. Per commit, render the first and revised single-plan briefs and exercise their missing-input and unfilled-slot refusals. Only in the full suite, exercise plan-critique rendering, filing, the pass form and their refusals, including a MINOR before a MAJOR with dismissal of F2, two blocking findings with only one dismissed, a revision that misses a first finding, and a committed critique whose working copy differs only by line-ending conversion or has a real uncommitted edit. Render the scope critique with every registry present and with one absent; render ordinary, absent-stance, and convergence review briefs in process and at the command line; prove their distinct reading scopes; refuse narrow convergence and unknown stance; file a convergence-tagged finding; check the live scope rubric against the rendered registry set and the plan-critique every-class and confirming-round reachability gates; pin single-plan registry lines; and, in the full suite, prove that SUCCESSOR never writes a plan-cap pass record.\"\r\ntier = \"Full\"\r\nexpected = \"A complete attended review or critique brief has no unfilled slot and carries the shipped review clauses, lane facts or scope rubric as applicable. An empty test list, invalid round shape, missing specification, unreadable or empty named input, unfilled template slot, malformed or unbound verdict (without exactly one line whose keyword, compared case-insensitively, is VERDICT in the canonical form `VERDICT: <APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED> findings=<digits>`), and a lane branch the round reader cannot read as a review scope are refused without a requested file or review record. A bracketed non-severity bullet beside N declared-severity findings and findings=N is filed, and its rollup shows N. A detached lane, non-commit revision or non-ancestor base returns exit 2 with no output file and a clean repository. A successful review returns exit 0 and writes only its brief with full resolved revisions and the reviewed tip; successful filing returns exit 0 and adds only the next review-round record. A valid filed review is rendered in the lane review history. Per-commit brief renders and their stated refusals remain in the smoke tier. The full suite proves that the first critique covers Done-when alone, the second also covers first-critique finding identifiers without their text, a revision missing a first finding is refused at render, a MINOR before a MAJOR passes on dismissal of F2, two blocking findings with only one dismissed are refused, and a committed critique changed only by line-ending conversion passes while a real uncommitted edit is refused. The second plan critique's brief never carries the first critique's verdict or any finding's text. A successfully filed critique writes only its record. A plan critique with a missing rubric, plan or specification, or a beyond delta, refuses without output. The shipped planner template is in the catalogue. The scope critique names the five declared registries and marks only an absent one; absent stance and ordinary output match and contain no stance line; convergence has one fixed line after the range; narrow convergence refuses without a brief; a tagged finding files as one finding; and SUCCESSOR at the plan cap writes no pass record. An ordinary review reading scope includes the delegated-decisions record and filed plan critiques, while a convergence scope excludes both. A plan critique ties every severity class to reachability, counts a fail-open, data-loss, or security path only when a supported workflow or the kit's own routes reach it, and limits a rare or contrived path to MINOR at most or dismissal; its confirming round treats a new finding as BLOCKER or MAJOR only when it is a reachable fail-open, data-loss, or Done-when gap.\"\r\nautomated = \"Yes\"\r\nevidence = \"tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_rendered_review_brief_carries_the_kit_clauses_and_the_lane_facts; tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_narrow_round_names_the_findings_it_answers; tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_narrow_round_without_findings_and_a_full_one_with_them_are_refused; tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_named_input_that_is_not_there_refuses; tests/test_review_brief.py::test_an_unfilled_slot_refuses_and_no_brief_is_written; tests/test_review_brief.py::test_the_scope_critique_carries_ruling_twos_questions_and_its_rubric; tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_filed_coordinator_review_appears_in_the_rollup; tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_bracketed_word_that_is_not_a_severity_never_refuses_a_filing; tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_malformed_review_is_refused_and_nothing_is_filed; tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_round_with_no_tests_refuses; tests/test_review_brief.py::test_an_empty_or_missing_named_input_refuses; tests/test_review_brief.py::test_the_critique_render_refuses_a_missing_spec; tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_the_command_line_refuses_a_lane_the_git_reads_reject; tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_a_review_render_exits_zero_and_writes_only_the_brief; tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_filing_a_review_exits_zero_and_writes_only_the_round_file; tests/test_plan_pass.py::test_the_planner_brief_carries_the_clauses_the_contract_and_the_delta_question; tests/test_plan_pass.py::test_the_revision_brief_asks_the_delta_question_again_and_labels_its_inputs; tests/test_plan_pass.py::test_the_planner_brief_refuses_a_missing_input; tests/test_plan_pass.py::test_the_planner_brief_refuses_an_unfilled_slot; tests/test_plan_pass.py::test_the_command_writes_nothing_when_the_brief_refuses; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_a_critique_outside_the_verdict_format_is_refused_and_nothing_is_written; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_a_plan_with_a_missing_or_beyond_delta_is_never_filed; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_the_pass_form_refuses_while_head_lacks_the_critique_it_cites; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_the_pass_form_reads_a_committed_critique_through_line_ending_conversion; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_a_plan_the_coverage_gate_fails_never_passes; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_a_minor_only_change_request_passes; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_a_first_change_request_allows_one_revision_and_never_passes; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_at_the_cap_an_accepted_dismissal_of_every_blocking_finding_passes; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_at_the_cap_anything_but_an_accepted_dismissal_is_refused; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_the_plan_critique_renders_over_the_committed_plan_and_its_coverage; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_the_second_plan_critique_is_in_convergence_and_never_sees_the_first; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_at_the_cap_a_major_after_a_minor_is_disputed_under_its_own_number; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_at_the_cap_two_blocking_findings_with_one_dismissed_are_refused; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_the_dispute_findings_file_refuses_ids_other_than_the_blocking_ones; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_the_second_critique_refuses_a_revision_that_misses_a_first_finding; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_a_filed_critique_exits_zero_and_writes_only_its_record; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_at_the_cap_an_uncommitted_dispute_verdict_is_refused; tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_the_plan_critique_refuses_a_missing_input_and_writes_nothing; tests/test_plan_pass.py::test_the_planner_template_is_in_the_generated_catalogue; tests/test_plan_pass.py::test_the_shipped_planner_template_names_the_architecture_skill_by_its_agent_paths_and_no_row_of_this_repo;tests/test_plan_pass.py::test_the_planner_brief_names_each_input_by_its_registry;tests/test_review_brief.py::test_the_scope_critique_names_the_registries_beside_the_spec;tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_convergence_round_renders_the_one_fixed_stance_line;tests/test_review_brief.py::test_an_absent_stance_renders_the_ordinary_round;tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_narrow_round_refuses_the_convergence_stance;tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_convergence_brief_carries_the_stance_and_its_marking;tests/test_review_brief.py::test_a_convergence_tagged_finding_files_with_its_count_unchanged;tests/test_review_brief.py::test_the_scope_rubric_names_every_registry_the_critique_brief_renders;tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_at_the_cap_a_successor_ruling_never_passes;tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_the_reading_scope_never_shows_a_record_of_earlier_findings;tests/test_review_brief.py::test_only_a_convergence_brief_leaves_the_decisions_record_and_critiques_out_of_its_reading_scope;tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_an_ordinary_review_reads_the_decisions_record;tests/test_plan_filing.py::test_the_plan_critique_brief_ties_severity_to_reachability\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\nphase = 6\r\n\r\n[test.TC-371]\r\nverifies = [\"SR-241\", \"LLR-341\", \"IF-288\", \"IF-289\"]\r\nlevel = \"Integration\"\r\nmethod = \"Exercise review rendering on real lane commits with no review record; records naming another tree or parent and assert each refusal names both values; malformed records, another bar, and another named test set; a matching record; record-only commits above a matching record; work commits above it; and record-only commits above an attested commit, 63 of them (the attested commit is the 64th the search examines) and then 65. Exercise a record written by the attestation route and render with an equivalent reordered test list.\"\r\ntier = \"Full\"\r\nexpected = \"Review rendering refuses with no brief for every absent or invalid record and names the reviewed commit, its cause and the recording step; another-bar and test-set refusals name both compared values; a tree-or-parent refusal names both values; and a search that finds no verified record among the first 64 commits it examines, the reviewed commit included, refuses naming the bound, while one that meets the record as the 64th renders. A matching record, including one beneath only record-only commits, renders and reports the attested commit and digest; a work commit above the record refuses. The record producer and rendering consumer agree on the derived test set.\"\r\nautomated = \"Yes\"\r\nevidence = \"tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_a_tip_with_no_attestation_refuses_naming_the_attest_command; tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_an_attestation_that_does_not_name_the_tip_refuses_naming_the_cause; tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_a_landing_attestation_refuses_naming_both_bars; tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_a_review_attestation_for_other_tests_refuses_naming_both_digests; tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_a_matching_review_attestation_renders_and_names_it; tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_record_only_commits_above_the_attested_one_render; tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_a_work_commit_above_the_attested_one_refuses; tests/test_review_brief_git.py::test_a_search_past_the_walk_bound_refuses; tests/test_integrate_attest.py::test_the_review_render_accepts_what_attest_writes\"\r\nstatus = \"Approved\"\r\nphase = 6\r\n\r\n[design.LLR-367]\r\nsr_refs = [\"SR-241\"]\r\ntitle = \"Derive a lane review test set from its requirement trace\"\r\nmodule = \"project-trajectory/scripts/review_set.py;project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/spine.py\"\r\ncode_symbol = \"derive/ReviewSet/llrs_under/review_scope\"\r\ndetail = \"The derivation reads one claimed work-item copy, using its closed copy when available, then its requirement references, merge base, changed paths, declared paths, registries, and test declarations. Its scope is the row's referenced system requirements, every low-level requirement referring to them, and every test case of any status verifying either. Each scoped test case is exactly one of tagged, declared non-executable, untagged executable, or no evidence. It returns sorted tagged test files, listed non-test evidence, and every gap. A code-touching row with no requirement reference refuses by name; one with gaps refuses partial naming every gap; one with no tagged file and no gap refuses empty; a row not touching code reports gaps without refusal and permits an empty set. Missing, multiple, unreadable, malformed, or inconsistent inputs refuse by name; an unreadable input outranks partial and empty. The derivation never widens to the whole bar.\"\r\nrationale = \"One closed derivation prevents recording, drawing, and rendering from disagreeing about required verification while preserving named, repairable refusals.\"\r\ntest_refs = \"TC-413; TC-414; TC-415; TC-416; TC-417; TC-418; TC-419; TC-420\"\r\nstatus = \"Drafted\"\r\ncomponent = \"CMP-008\"\r\nphase = 6\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
